it is a pleasure to welcome back to the  program Sean Carroll who is a  theoretical physicist at Caltech host of  the podcast mindscape and also author of  the forthcoming book something deeply  hidden which is out in September maybe  we start there Sean I mean what is it  with this idea of something being hidden  is very interesting when we talk about  your work what is it that you allude to  there as being hidden from us well it's  a quote from Einstein actually and the  book is about quantum mechanics and the  fact that for the whole 20th century  physicists kind of punted the attempt to  truly understand what was going on in  quantum mechanics so something deeply  hidden refers to Einstein's conviction  which I share that there's something  going on behind the operation of the  natural world when we talk about quantum  mechanics we've often not really pressed  our best theoretical theories on what is  happening we make a quantum measurement  I think it's time to actually face this  problem head-on why would it be that we  don't press as much as we might in that  area well it's hard you know the quantum  mechanics is mysterious mostly because  it seems to imply that what truly exists  the true nature of physical reality is  different from what we see when we look  at it right there's a difference between  measurement outcomes and physical  reality and that's something that no  other physical theory has ever featured  so way back in the 1920s and 30s  physicists settled on a coping mechanism  which they called the Copenhagen  interpretation which asked us to sort of  not ask too carefully what happens when  you make a quantum measurement and I  think that we can do better than that by  now how do you explain to laypeople not  only in the simplest possible terms what  what quantum mechanics is but also why  it even matters because I think  sometimes there's a contingent that when  faced with something as complicated as  quantum mechanics it can be easy to say  if I don't even understand why that  matters to me or why that matters to  human life I'm not even gonna think  about it too much like why is this stuff  important  yeah quantum mechanics is mysterious  because  were useful like seeing particles right  we say that there's a particle it has a  position it has a velocity and quantum  mechanics says that's not what is really  happening particles have a wavefunction  and you can observe their position you  can observe their velocity but that's  not what is really going on and  nevertheless quantum mechanics is at the  center of all of modern physics it is  the most important theory we have and it  is incredibly important to understand  things like atoms electricity  transistors lasers all of modern  technology is based on quantum mechanics  in one way or another to me the most  vivid fact about quantum mechanics is  that without it matter would be  completely unstable electrons would not  stay orbiting the nuclei of atoms the  chair you're sitting on would not be  solid it would all just collapse into a  black hole in a tiny fraction of a  second so quantum mechanics is kind of  really important to how we understand  the universe right now so to play  pseudo-intellectual devil's advocate is  it important to understand those things  for the layperson  as long as they work you know you don't  understand anything really to get  through life except you know how to eat  and how to sleep and so forth but I  think that what makes us human is  something very different right I think  that human beings like to do more than  simply survive of course you can get  through your daily life as long as your  daily life is not that of being a  physicist without knowing that much  about quantum mechanics but it is the  best theory we have of the fundamental  nature of reality and it is  understandable so if you give in to the  human impulse to be curious to  understand the world around us then  understanding quantum mechanics should  be very high on your list of priorities  yeah very very well set can you talk a  little bit about some of the ways in  which a better understanding of quantum  mechanics could be a game-changer  over the next I mean whatever timeline  you you think is relevant I mean whether  it's 20 years or 50 years or a hundred  years in what ways will a better  understanding of quantum mechanics  change the way in which humans interact  with the world around us in our  environment well I think there's two big  waves it's you know it's very  interesting to imagine that we've been  using quantum mechanics for 90 years or  so  and it's our best theory of physics and  yet we don't understand it there are  other things we also don't understand  for example we don't understand how  gravity fits into the quantum mechanical  paradigm and no one seems to have I had  the idea that maybe the fact that we  don't understand quantum gravity is  related to the fact that we don't  understand quantum mechanics itself so I  think that understanding quantum  mechanics itself will be really really  helpful in our pursuit of a theory of  everything of once and for all final  theory of the way the world works at a  much more down-to-earth level technology  is improving to the point where quantum  mechanical effects are becoming manifest  in the devices that we build we're  building not only quantum computers but  quantum cryptography quantum  eavesdropping a whole bunch of  applications of quantum mechanics and so  we're going to be needing to know what  the rules are of the quantum world we're  gonna need to do better than the kind of  slapdash recipe we put together in the  1920s can you talk a little bit about  what the many worlds theory is well when  you make a measurement of a quantum  system the system can be in what is  called a superposition it's neither here  nor there it's in the superposition of  every possible place it could be but  when we look at it we never see a  superposition we see every single  possible measurement outcome but only  one at a time and so what many-worlds  says is what you're missing the trick is  to realize that you yourself are a  quantum mechanical system that means  that you can be in super positions so  when you measure a particle and it could  have many different possible locations  it's not according to many worlds that  once you observe it it collapses to a  single location rather the particle is  still in many possible locations but you  are in many possible measurement  outcomes there's a world where the  particle was there and you saw it there  there's another world where the particle  is over here and you saw it over here  and this is a straightforward prediction  of the equations of quantum mechanics it  sort of been lurking in implicitly in  the formalism since the Schrodinger  equation came along in the 1920s and we  just need to face up to that reality now  is this for  people who are hearing and saying that  is this a multiverse sort of theory the  idea that I'm married to person a in the  world in which you know you and I are  currently speaking but there's another  world in which I'm not married there's a  third world in which I'm a tiger I mean  you know however far you want to take it  and sort of cartoonish is it concurrent  with that idea or is it actually  something a little different it is a  version of that idea absolutely but it's  not just that there is every possible  universe there are certain universes the  ones that arise because of how the laws  of physics play out and there are not  other universes but there's a huge  challenge that is raised by many worlds  because there's the notion of personal  identity that we have I mean in the  sentence you just said you said is there  a world in which I am a tiger okay so no  there's no world in which you are a  tiger there might be another world in  which other things happen that created a  tiger out of the same descendants that  you had right or there's another world  in which there's a version very similar  to you in which you had something  different for dinner last night or  married a different person or something  like that but they're not you these  people in the other worlds are all  separate every person in a world is an  individual and all the individuals are  different people so I think sometimes  when people think about the multiverse  theories they share an identity with  that quote other version of themselves  that is in some of these other universes  that seems as I hear you saying it may  be a slightly overly simplistic way of  thinking about it yeah I think that you  know the way the philosophers would say  it is we need to update our metaphysics  in order to take into account how many  worlds works if I'm gonna measure the  spin of an electron and I picked that  because there's only two possible  outcomes it's spinning counterclockwise  or it's spinning clockwise right so then  there will be two people afterward one  person saw it's been clockwise the other  saw it's been counterclockwise and it's  very very tempting for us to say before  we do the measurement I wonder which one  I'm going to be but the correct answer  is there's no answer to that question  there is no one that you're going to be  you are now one person you will have two  descendants a few moments from now they  be separate people they'll be related to  you in the same way that an ordinary  older version of yourself is related to  the younger version of yourself it's  just that there are multiple copies of  them it's a different but completely  sensible way of thinking about reality  so along these lines I want to talk  about entropy a little bit one analogy  that I have found useful in talking  about entropy is to explain and I'm  curious your feedback on this as a  science educator the idea that if you  build a house and leave it there and  leave it subject to weather and  everything else that is going on around  it there could be a hurricane that will  knock some pieces off of it and it might  snow a lot and that would damage pieces  of the roof which would fall off and  eventually the house would basically be  rubble you are not going to then have  those same or other forces happen to get  the house back into the built condition  in which it started is that a useful  analogy to think about entropy yeah no  that's an that's not just an analogy  that's an example of entropy in action  there you go and so when we think about  that as a concept more broadly not  applied to a house but apply to our  universe what are the sort of important  aspects of how and why it matters well  that nothing matters more to reality  into life and to our existence here on  earth than the fact that the entropy the  disorderliness of the universe was very  very low near the Big Bang fourteen  billion years ago and has been rising  ever since the fact that we're all born  young and we grow older with time we age  that's ultimately because entropy is  increasing the fact that you remember  the past and you don't remember the  future that's ultimately because entropy  is increasing the fact that you can make  a choice right now that will affect what  happens in the future but you cannot  make a choice right now that affected  what did happen in the past all is  because entropy is increasing so that  may seem significant sort of to the  individual life but why is that so  significant when we think about the past  and future of the universe well we think  we're not absolutely sure about this but  we think that the fundamental laws of  physics don't actually distinguish  between the past and future if you look  at Newton's laws that we sometimes learn  in our physics courses or if you look at  the Schrodinger equation of quantum  mechanics or Einstein's equation of  general relativity none of them have a  preference for one direction of time  over the other they treat them equally  but the real world seems to have a very  strong preference and we think that the  origin of that strong preference in the  real world is because entropy was low so  if it wasn't for that difference if it  wasn't for the fact that entropy used to  be low we would treat the past and  future on completely equal grounds we  would not remember the past any more  than we currently remember the future  absolutely fascinating as Sean what who  should read the forthcoming book  something deeply hidden and and what in  addition to some of the things we've  touched on will be it will be part of  that book yeah it's for everyone you  know does not assume that you know any  physics it does assume that you're  willing to stretch your mind a little  bit right these ideas are you know it's  not heavily mathematical there's no  equations in the book but they're very  very different from our everyday lives  so it asks not expertise of the reader  but patience and imagination and a  willingness to sort of sit back and  really really think about the  fundamental nature of reality and I'll  talk about quantum mechanics about many  worlds and then at the end of the book I  talk about quantum gravity and how  space-time itself might emerge from a  quantum mechanical wave function of the  universe the book is out in September it  is by Sean Carroll it is called  something deeply hidden Sean so great to  talk to you again appreciate you making  time to talk to me thank you very much  David

